[Fix PGXP Stretched Polygons]
Description = Fixes stretched polygons close to the camera.
Author = Stenzek
Type = Gameshark
Activation = EndFrame
; The world vertex projection loop (around 80028C54) has a precision path. When all three
; components of a vertex are within roughly +/-128, it shifts them left by 4 (x16) before RTPS,
; sets a flag in a2, and later shifts the resulting SZ3 right by 4 for the ordering table. With
; TR=(0,0,0) the scaled vertex projects to the same screen X/Y, so on real hardware the only
; effect is 4 extra bits of integer precision in IR1/IR2/SZ3.
;
; PGXP attaches the RTPS depth to each SXY for perspective-correct texturing. It has no way to
; see the game's later SZ3 >> 4, so near vertices carry a depth 16x larger than their unscaled
; neighbours. Polygons that mix the two (for example the ground just in front of the camera) get
; inconsistent per-vertex depths, which shows up as badly stretched or swimming textures.
;
; This patch removes the x16 scale and the matching >> 4 and leaves the flag logic alone, so the
; 0x2000 "near" bit in the stored SZ word is still set as before:
;   80028C98  sll a0, v1, 20  ->  sll a0, v1, 16   (keep the halfword packing, drop the x16)
;   80028C9C  sll at, at, 4   ->  nop
;   80028CA0  sll v0, v0, 4   ->  nop
;   80028CD4  srl a0, a0, 4   ->  nop
; The ordering-table depth is essentially unchanged (floor(z) instead of floor(16z) >> 4).
;
; Trade-off: near vertices lose the 4 extra bits of integer precision. With PGXP's "Preserve
; Projection Precision" (PGXPPreserveProjFP) enabled, the projection is done in floating point
; from the raw inputs, so nothing is lost. Without it, near geometry may wobble slightly at high
; upscale factors.
;
; 80028C98: sll a0, v1, 20 (00032500) -> sll a0, v1, 16 (00032400)
A7028C98 25002400
; 80028C9C: sll at, at, 4 (00010900) -> nop
A7028C9C 09000000
A7028C9E 00010000
; 80028CA0: sll v0, v0, 4 (00021100) -> nop
A7028CA0 11000000
A7028CA2 00020000
; 80028CD4: srl a0, a0, 4 (00042102) -> nop
A7028CD4 21020000
A7028CD6 00040000


